meta {
  name: Deactivate a book
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

delete {
  url: {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  200, and it deactivates rather than deletes - which is why this is the last request in the
  folder rather than a destructive one you have to avoid.

  The response carries preservedLoanCount: the number of loans the row was kept for. Nothing
  is erased, and nothing can be: the loan's foreign key is RESTRICT, so the database itself
  refuses to delete a book with history no matter what code asks it to.

  409 while copies are still out on loan. Closing a title while readers hold copies would
  leave those loans pointing at a catalogue entry that no longer accepts returns - return or
  cancel them first.

  Repeating the call is a 200 with alreadyInactive: true and nothing written. A caller
  repeating the request has the outcome they asked for and there is nothing for them to fix.

  ## Why this is not in Books

  It belongs there by subject, and it is here by order. Deactivating is refused while copies
  are out, so a full `bru run -r` with this request sitting seventh in Books would withdraw
  the title before the Loans folder ever borrowed from it - and every loan below would then
  answer 409. Putting it last is what makes the collection runnable end to end.
}
